;;; A written `eq` fact is stored, not checked against its definition —
;;; regression `fact-eq-false` (`corpus/regressions.toml`) (Q-M1e2.26:
;;; `eq` and `neq` are relations whose facts are fixed by name identity,
;;; computed and never stored, and no fact, rule or commitment changes them; a
;;; rule may conclude a false one, which is a contradiction).
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and the written fact `(eq a w)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A fact is a conclusion with no premise, so a written fact of `eq` is checked
;;; as a rule's conclusion of one is (Q-M1e2.26; Q-M1f.33): `a` and `w` are two
;;; names, so `(eq a w)` is false, and root is a contradiction, `(false)`
;;; written where it stands. R is empty. Since nothing is stored, `w` is in no
;;; fact, and no name of the program: H is empty.
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The fact is stored: root holds `(eq a w)` (`ein saturate FILE --dump`), `w`
;;; is a name of the program, so `(p w)` is a candidate, and R is two states, ∅
;;; and `{(p w)}`, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at
;;; `dc4c0bdc`, release and debug alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?3} is a node")
(p a)
(eq a w)
